diff --git a/I386-PORT.md b/I386-PORT.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..82dde4fe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/I386-PORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+# Genuine Intel 80386 port
+
+This branch ports glibc 2.41 to the original Intel 80386 instruction set. It
+preserves the ordinary i386 GNU/Linux ABI, but it requires a small versioned
+kernel atomic service because an 80386 has neither `CMPXCHG` nor `XADD`.
+
+The implementation was developed with the Linux 7.1 PC-98 port in
+`awemorris/linux-pc98`, but no PC-98 hardware behavior is built into glibc.
+Another i386 Linux port can use it if its kernel implements the same UAPI.
+
+## Configure contract
+
+An exact `--host=i386-linux-gnu` build must explicitly pass:
+
+```sh
+--enable-i386-kernel-atomics
+```
+
+The supplied Linux headers must define both `I386_ATOMIC_ABI_VERSION == 1`
+and `__NR_i386_atomic`. Configure fails closed when either is missing. The
+option is rejected for i486 and newer builds, which continue to use glibc's
+ordinary native-atomic sysdeps.
+
+The validated first build also uses `--disable-multi-arch` and:
+
+```sh
+CFLAGS='-O2 -g -march=i386 -mtune=i386 -fno-omit-frame-pointer'
+```
+
+The top-level linux-pc98 scripts `build-glibc.sh`,
+`build-glibc-tests.sh`, `build-glibc-busybox.sh`, and
+`check-glibc-i386-opcodes.sh` provide the reproducible build and validation
+workflow.
+
+## Kernel ABI
+
+ABI version 1 accepts this request through syscall 472 in the PC-98 Linux
+i386 syscall table:
+
+```c
+struct i386_atomic_args {
+        uint32_t version;
+        uint32_t operation;
+        uint32_t width;
+        uint32_t address;
+        uint32_t expected;
+        uint32_t value;
+        uint32_t observed;
+        uint32_t reserved;
+};
+```
+
+The service supports naturally aligned 1-, 2-, and 4-byte CMPXCHG, XADD,
+XCHG, AND, OR, and XOR. It returns zero on success and writes the old target
+value to `observed`. Kernel and glibc source must be upgraded together if the
+ABI changes.
+
+The Linux implementation is restricted to the uniprocessor `M386` kernel.
+The public syscall pins writable pages to honor COW and read-only mappings on
+a CPU whose supervisor writes ignore read-only PTEs. The kernel also routes
+its i386 futex atomic helpers through the same serialized implementation.
+
+## Source layout
+
+- `sysdeps/i386/i386-kernel-atomic/isa.h` reports the 386 ISA floor.
+- `sysdeps/i386/i386-kernel-atomic/dl-tlsdesc.S` provides the FNSAVE TLS
+  descriptor resolver and avoids later FPU/SSE state instructions.
+- `sysdeps/i386/i386-kernel-atomic/pthread_spin_trylock.S` implements trylock
+  with the implicitly locked memory form of `XCHG`.
+- `sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/i386/i386-kernel-atomic/atomic-machine.h` is the
+  exact-i386 atomic backend.
+- `sysdeps/i386/preconfigure` selects this leaf only for exact i386.
+- `sysdeps/x86/cpu-features.c` bypasses CPUID and later CPU dispatch for this
+  build.
+- `sysdeps/i386/nptl/pthread_spin_lock.S` avoids `PAUSE` (`REP NOP`) in the
+  exact-i386 build.
+- `sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/i386/arch-syscall.h` records syscall 472.
+
+Both `configure.ac` and generated `configure` files are updated in this
+branch. Keep generated files synchronized when changing configure logic.
+
+`manual/Makefile` is a minimal no-op subdirectory makefile because Debian's
+repacked source archive omits the GFDL manual while the upstream top-level
+build still descends into `manual/`.
+
+## Atomic design
+
+Memory `XCHG` is available on the 80386 and is used directly for exchange and
+spin-lock operations. Locked arithmetic which does not need to return the old
+value also remains native. Operations whose glibc algorithms require the old
+value or a compare-and-exchange use the kernel service.
+
+The syscall wrapper exchanges EBX with a callee-saved ESI operand around
+`INT 80h`; this preserves the i386 PIC GOT register and works in libc, ld.so,
+static PIE, and non-PIC objects. Any kernel ABI failure triggers `INT3`
+instead of silently violating NPTL atomicity.
+
+64-bit atomics remain unavailable, matching the normal 32-bit i386 ABI
+constraints. A new glibc use which requires a 64-bit lock-free atomic should
+fail at link time and must be reviewed rather than silently emulated.
+
+## Validated behavior
+
+The port has run under qemu-pc98 with `-M pc9801 -cpu 386` and 32 MiB RAM.
+A dynamically glibc-linked BusyBox runs as `/sbin/init`. The validation suite
+passes dynamic loading, malloc/free, libm `dlopen`/`dlsym`, TLS, pthread
+mutexes, fork, process-shared robust owner-death recovery, and a 12-test
+kernel atomic suite including read-only and COW fault containment.
+
+Every executable section in the installed loader, shared libraries, test
+programs, and dynamic BusyBox has also passed the top-level post-386 opcode
+scanner. The same scan verifies that no compiler `__atomic_*` or `__sync_*`
+helper remains unresolved. The source builds and boots successfully as i486
+without the kernel-atomic sysdeps leaf.
+
+## Remaining production gates
+
+This is a working executable port, not yet a Debian archive-ready libc. The
+full upstream glibc testsuite, exact Debian 13 patch stack, ABI/symbol-version
+comparison, Debian package build, install/upgrade tests, broad NSS/locale/
+resolver coverage, and physical 80386 validation remain required. See
+`GLIBC-2.41-I386-PORT-PLAN.md` in the linux-pc98 repository for the complete
+test matrix and risk register.
